---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About HomeMaterialCalc.com — Free Material Estimators for US Homeowners"
  description="HomeMaterialCalc.com helps US homeowners and DIYers calculate how much material to buy — in real purchasable units — before heading to the store."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-6">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Home</a> › <span class="text-gray-700">About</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">About HomeMaterialCalc.com</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p>
        HomeMaterialCalc.com is a free collection of material estimators built for US homeowners
        and DIYers who are planning a landscaping or construction project and want to know
        <em>exactly how much to buy</em> before they head to the store or call a supplier.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do</h2>
      <p>
        Our calculators take your actual project dimensions — length, width, depth, and shape —
        and instantly convert them into real purchasable units: bags, cubic yards, tons, pallets,
        or pieces. No guessing, no rounding to some number that feels about right.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every result also shows you the step-by-step calculation breakdown so you can verify the
        math yourself or explain it to a supplier.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we built this</h2>
      <p>
        Material estimating is one of those tasks that sounds simple but trips up a lot of people.
        How many bags of mulch do you need for a bed that is 15 feet wide and not quite a
        rectangle? How many pallets of sod for a lawn with an irregular shape? How many 80-pound
        bags of Quikrete for a slab that is 4 inches thick?
      </p>
      <p>
        The math is straightforward — area, volume, a few constants — but pulling it all together
        in your head while standing in a parking lot is error-prone. We built clean, fast
        calculators so you can get an accurate answer in seconds.
      </p>

      <h2>Our commitment to accuracy</h2>
      <p>
        Calculation accuracy is the entire point of this site. We use a single source-of-truth
        constants file that is documented with sources on our <a href="/methodology">Methodology page</a>.
        We run automated tests on every formula so a code change cannot silently break the math.
        If you find an error, please <a href="/contact">let us know</a> — we take it seriously.
      </p>

      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <p>
        The current site covers the most-requested outdoor materials: mulch, gravel, topsoil,
        sand, sod, and concrete. We plan to expand to indoor materials — flooring, tile, paint,
        drywall, and insulation — once the outdoor section is well-established.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions, corrections, or suggestions? Use our <a href="/contact">contact form</a>.
        We read every message.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
